  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/816c4ecbfe49321bc6b2bc0d72eb61d6ddd1aeae7e76a275df0975439dde5865.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$",
  "worked_solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/6782f2fa59056afd9022edbaf69c44a991bf12a7ac6a190bea3cc08fc7a4996c.png)\n\nNote that in $\u25b3OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2.5|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2.5b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+2.5b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+2.5b.$\n\nSince $a\u2226b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2.5\n$$",
